**Comparison summary for "eating healthier":** A stricter/vegetarian-leaning pescatarian diet (minimal dairy/eggs) maximizes plant-food benefits similar to vegetarian/vegan diets but risks missing some nutrients unless fish intake is regular; a fuller pescatarian diet (including dairy, eggs, and varied seafood) is the most nutritionally complete and flexible of the three plant-forward diets, delivering omega-3s, B12, protein, and minerals with the lowest risk of deficiency — but moderation and fish variety are needed to limit mercury exposure from large predatory fish.

ANSWER: {"answer": "Visited healthline.com and compared pescatarian diet information across two Healthline articles (no sign-in/account/payment/personal data needed). 'What Is a Pescatarian Diet?' explains the core diet: plant-based foods (grains, legumes, nuts, fruits, vegetables) plus fish/seafood and often eggs/dairy, excluding all meat and poultry; benefits include lower diabetes/heart-disease risk, more omega-3s and protein; main drawback is mercury in large fish (limit swordfish, shark, king mackerel, tilefish, tuna). 'Vegetarian vs. Vegan vs. Pescatarian' compares pescatarian directly to vegetarian and vegan diets: all three cut red meat/poultry and share heart-health/cancer-risk benefits, but pescatarian is the most flexible and nutritionally complete (seafood supplies omega-3s, iodine, B12 that plant-only diets often lack), vegetarian still gets some nutrients from dairy/eggs, and vegan is most restrictive with the highest risk of nutritional deficiencies needing careful planning/supplements. Conclusion: a pescatarian diet that includes varied seafood plus dairy/eggs is the healthiest, most nutrient-complete and flexible option of the two compared, as long as mercury-heavy fish are limited, whereas a stricter near-vegetarian pescatarian approach gains plant-diet benefits but needs regular fish intake to avoid nutrient gaps."}
